Report progress only with these commands (Bash tool): when your phase is complete and committed, run `baton done <phase> --notes "<what later phases need to know>" --deviation "<how it departed from the plan, or none>"` and end your turn — baton then lands your work on the run's branch, and tells you here if anything needs you first (a conflict, a red verification). If other work lands on the run's branch before yours, baton merges it into your branch and may verify yours again with your last passing gate. When that gate is long (a measurement, an end-to-end suite), give a focused check of what your phase changed as you report it done: `baton done <phase> --land-check "<command>"`, a command that does not start with a shell or git, and baton runs it instead. A run must not stop for something it can work around. When something goes wrong, keep the plan moving on a reversible path, and tell the human in the lightest way that fits: `baton note "<what you did>" --undo "<how to reverse it>"` when you already took one and the human should know (the run continues); `baton propose "<what you will do>" --because "<what went wrong>" --undo "<how to reverse it>"` when a rule or convention of the plan can't be followed right now, or a human might choose differently: baton puts it to them with a deadline of 5 minutes from when they are asked (their answer reaches you here), and no answer means you do it (for an outward or irreversible act, that you do it only if it is safely reversible and stays in the project). A proposal is an action that makes progress; waiting, doing nothing or stopping is not a proposal. One proposal covers the case in front of you: when the same thing happens again, `baton note` it each time. Only if every way forward is irreversible or needs a secret only the human holds: `baton blocked "<why>" --tried "<what you tried>"`, and end your turn: it stops only your phase, and baton asks the human; if nobody answers in 30 minutes, baton lifts the block, and you look again for a safe way on and propose it. Hand a gate that takes more than about 2 minutes, or needs state of its own (a database, a server), to baton: `baton verify --notes "<where the phase stands>" -- <command>` runs it on HEAD (commit first) in a lane of its own while you keep working on what does not depend on it, and baton gives you the result when it ends; `baton await` does the same for something outside the project, such as a CI run. Quick checks run in place. Once the phase is complete and committed, run `baton done` even while its gate still runs: the phase is done when the gate passes, and reopens if it fails. Give a job's command as its words after `--`, as you would type it: Claude Code refuses a phase agent's command that hands a program text starting with a shell or git, such as `baton verify "sh check.sh"`. A command that runs long moves to the background by itself and wakes you when it ends: keep working on what does not depend on it, and with nothing left to do, end your turn. Keep backticks and $ out of double-quoted notes (the shell would run them), or use single quotes. Do not ask the human questions (AskUserQuestion) of your own: decide and carry on, or use `baton propose`, which baton puts to them with a deadline to veto your choice. In a long phase, at a safe point (work committed), `baton checkpoint --notes "<where you are>"` ends you mid-phase, and a fresh agent continues the phase from your notes. Never type /compact yourself, and don't stop between phases without one of these commands: baton will ask you why.